        <p class="profile-detail-region">Life · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Aristotle</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">384 BCE – 322 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">384 BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stagira">Stagira</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcidice">Chalcidice</a>, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>; son of Nicomachus, personal physician to King Amyntas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, and Phaestis, whose family came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">322 BCE at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, of natural causes, after fleeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> when he was reportedly denounced for impiety</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Philosopher and polymath; member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s Academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> for nearly twenty years (to 348/47 BCE); tutor to Alexander, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> (from 343/42 BCE); founder and head of the Peripatetic school in the Lyceum, Athens, for twelve years (from about 335 BCE)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the earliest known systematic study of logic; founding the Peripatetic school in the Lyceum; tutoring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>; the first systematic study of biology; the four causes and the theory of matter and form; the Nicomachean Ethics, Politics and Poetics</span></div>
        </div>
