    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A physician's son from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stagira">Stagira</a>, Aristotle spent nearly twenty years at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s Academy, tutored the young Alexander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, and then founded his own school in the Lyceum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</li><li>His surviving treatises, generally thought to be lecture aids rather than works for publication, cover logic, metaphysics, physics, biology, ethics, politics, rhetoric and poetry; only around a third of what he wrote survives.</li><li>His logic remained dominant in the West until the 19th century and his physical science was not replaced systematically until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, and writers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-russell">Bertrand Russell</a> have by turns revered and attacked him.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Aristotle described the hectocotyl arm that cephalopods use in sexual reproduction; his description was widely disbelieved until the 19th century.</p></aside>
    </section>

