    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">384 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stagira">Stagira</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcidice">Chalcidice</a>, the son of Nicomachus, physician to King Amyntas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, and Phaestis</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 347 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s death, having spent nearly twenty years at the Academy, and goes with Xenocrates to Assos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">343 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Invited to Pella by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> to tutor his thirteen-year-old son Alexander</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">335 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, rents the Lyceum and establishes his own school there, later known as the Peripatetic school</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">322 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Reportedly denounced for impiety by Demophilus and Eurymedon the Hierophant, he flees <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> for his mother's family estate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">322 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Dies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, of natural causes, leaving his works to Theophrastus, his successor as head of the Lyceum</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/plato"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plato.jpg" alt="Plato" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plato_Silanion_Musei_Capitolini_MC1377.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marie-Lan Nguyen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Aristotle was born in 384 BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stagira">Stagira</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcidice">Chalcidice</a>, about 55 km east of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>. His father, Nicomachus, was the personal physician of King Amyntas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>; his mother, Phaestis, had origins in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>. Nicomachus was said to have belonged to the medical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of the Asclepiadae and was likely responsible for his son's early interest in biology and medicine. Ancient tradition held that the family descended from the legendary physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asclepius">Asclepius</a> and his son Machaon.</p><p>The details of his life are not well established: the biographies written in antiquity are often speculative, and historians agree only on a few salient points. Both parents died while he was still young, and Proxenus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atarneus">Atarneus</a> became his guardian. Little information about his childhood survives, but he probably spent some time in the Macedonian capital, Pella, where he made his first connections with the Macedonian monarchy.</p><p>At the age of seventeen or eighteen he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> to continue his education at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s Academy. There he became distinguished as a researcher and lecturer, and his tutor Plato nicknamed him the "mind of the school". He probably experienced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusinian-mysteries">Eleusinian Mysteries</a>, of whose sights he wrote that "to experience is to learn". He remained in Athens for nearly twenty years, leaving only after Plato's death.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon.jpg" alt="Philip II of Macedon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phillip_II,_king_of_Macedonia,_Roman_copy_of_Greek_original,_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek,_Copenhagen_(36420294055).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Mortel from Riyadh, Saudi Arabia, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Aristotle left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 348/47 BCE, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s death. The traditional story records that he was disappointed with the Academy's direction once control passed to Plato's nephew Speusippus, though anti-Macedonian sentiment in Athens may also have influenced him. With Xenocrates he went to Assos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> at the invitation of a former fellow student, Hermias of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atarneus">Atarneus</a>, and stayed for a few years. At Assos, and later on the nearby island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a>, he and his colleague Theophrastus carried out extensive research in botany and marine biology. During this time he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythia">Pythias</a>, the adoptive daughter and niece of Hermias.</p><p>In 343/42 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> invited him to Pella as tutor to his thirteen-year-old son Alexander, a choice perhaps influenced by his family's ties to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty">Macedonian dynasty</a>. He taught at the private school of Mieza, on the royal estate near Pella; the lessons probably included ethics and politics as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, and he gave Alexander an annotated copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>. The tutorship probably lasted only a few years, since at about sixteen Alexander was appointed regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>.</p><p>A year after Philip's assassination in 336 BCE, Aristotle returned to Athens for the second and final time. As a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metic">metic</a> he could not own property there, so he rented a building known as the Lyceum and established his own school in it. Its colonnade, the peripatos, gave the school the name Peripatetic.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Aristotle conducted courses and research at the Lyceum for twelve years, lecturing to small groups and, with students such as Theophrastus, Eudemus and Aristoxenus, building a large library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, maps and museum objects. Between 335 and 323 BCE he is believed to have composed many of his works. The dialogues he wrote survive only in fragments; the surviving treatises, among them the Physics, Metaphysics, Nicomachean Ethics, Politics, On the Soul and Poetics, are generally thought to be lecture aids for his students.</p><p>With the Prior Analytics he is credited with the earliest systematic study of logic. In the Metaphysics he concluded that a particular substance is a combination of matter and form, a theory called hylomorphism, and he distinguished four causes: material, formal, efficient and final. He was the first person to study biology systematically: he spent two years observing the zoology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> and the surrounding seas and distinguished about 500 animal species, drawing on his own observations, on beekeepers and fishermen, and on travellers' accounts.</p><p>In ethics he taught that the aim of deliberate human action is eudaimonia, generally translated as "happiness", which requires good character and the virtuous mean between excess and deficiency. In the Politics he treated the city as a natural community and stated that "man is by nature a political animal". The Rhetoric sets out three kinds of appeal, ethos, pathos and logos, and the Poetics describes tragedy as an imitation of action arousing pity and fear.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei.jpg" alt="Galileo Galilei" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galileo_Galilei_(1564-1642)_RMG_BHC2700.tiff" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Justus Sustermans, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Aristotle's philosophy took shape partly against that of his teacher. Where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> held that universals exist separately from the things that participate in them, Aristotle maintained that they are encountered only when instantiated in particular things, and in On the Soul he criticised Plato's theory of the soul; 20th-century scholarship held that he had there misinterpreted Plato. He was rather critical of democracy, and his attitude towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> was strongly ethnocentric: in one famous example he counsels Alexander to be "a leader to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and a despot to the barbarians".</p><p>His science was corrected from antiquity onwards. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archimedes">Archimedes</a> corrected his theory that bodies move towards their natural resting places, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herophilus">Herophilus</a> of Chalcedon placed intelligence in the brain rather than, as Aristotle had, in the heart. John Philoponus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a> are said to have shown by experiment that a heavier object does not fall faster than a lighter one; Carlo Rovelli argues to the contrary that Aristotle's physics of motion is correct within its domain, that of bodies falling through a fluid such as air.</p><p>Modern critics have been sharper. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-russell">Bertrand Russell</a> claimed that "almost every serious intellectual advance has had to begin with an attack on some Aristotelian doctrine" and called his ethics "repulsive". In 1985 the biologist Peter Medawar wrote of a "farrago of hearsay, imperfect observation, wishful thinking and credulity". Zoologists have frequently mocked his errors, although modern observation has confirmed several of his more surprising claims.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>During his years at the Lyceum his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythia">Pythias</a> died, and Aristotle became involved with Herpyllis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stagira">Stagira</a>; they had a son, whom he named Nicomachus after his father. Alexander had deeply admired him, but near the end of the king's life the two became estranged, holding diverging opinions on the administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, the treatment of conquered populations such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, and philosophical questions. A widespread speculation in antiquity held that Aristotle played a role in Alexander's death; the only evidence is an unlikely claim made some six years afterwards.</p><p>Alexander's death rekindled anti-Macedonian sentiment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. In 322 BCE Demophilus and Eurymedon the Hierophant reportedly denounced Aristotle for impiety, and he fled to his mother's family estate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>. He was said to have remarked, "I will not allow the Athenians to sin twice against philosophy", a reference to the trial and execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>. He died at Chalcis of natural causes later that year, having named his student <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> as his chief executor and asked in his will to be buried next to his wife.</p><p>He left his works to Theophrastus, his successor as head of the Lyceum, who passed them to Neleus of Scepsis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>; there the papers remained hidden until the collector Apellicon bought them. Copies of the major works were meanwhile circulating in Athens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Only around a third of his original output survives.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/averroes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/averroes.jpg" alt="Averroes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Averroes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrea_di_bonaiuto,_apoteosi_di_san_tommaso_d%27aquino,_11_averro%C3%A8.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sailko, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Lyceum grew into the Peripatetic school, and the surviving treatises were compiled in the 1st century BCE by Andronicus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>. Commentary became the chief way of engaging with them, from Boethus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> to Alexander of Aphrodisias, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonists</a> made his logical and physical works an introduction to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>. After the pagan schools were closed by order of Justinian in 529, Byzantine study of him was primarily from a Christian perspective.</p><p>His works were revived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, where most were translated into Arabic and taken up by Al-Kindi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-farabi">Al-Farabi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Ibn Sina</a> (Avicenna) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Averroes</a>; medieval Muslim scholars called him the "First Teacher". Moses <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a> based his Guide for the Perplexed on Aristotelianism. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> West he was practically unknown from c. 600 to c. 1100 except through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boethius">Boethius</a>'s translation of the Organon; translations from Arabic and Greek in the twelfth and thirteenth centuries revived interest, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> called him "The Philosopher".</p><p>His conception of logic was dominant in the West until 19th-century advances in mathematical logic, and his physical science was not replaced systematically until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a> wrote in 1882 that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-linnaeus">Linnaeus</a> and Cuvier "were mere schoolboys to old Aristotle". His ethics has gained renewed interest with modern virtue ethics, and Niko Tinbergen's four questions about animal behaviour are based on his four causes. Jonathan Barnes wrote that "an account of Aristotle's intellectual afterlife would be little less than a history of European thought".</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Aristotle (384–322 BCE), born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stagira">Stagira</a>, studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s Academy, tutored Alexander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and founded the Peripatetic school in the Lyceum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. His treatises on logic, nature, ethics and politics shaped medieval Islamic, Jewish and Christian scholarship, and his logic and physics held their place into modern times.</em></p>

